This tutorial walks through how to build a very simple login and register page using Firebase.  
It assumes you already know the basics of React, how components work, and how to set up a starter React project.

---

![Login/Register Screenshot](https://terrynrivera.me/cdn/blog/firebaselogin.png)

## 1. Install Firebase

Run the following command in your React project:

```
npm install firebase
```

---

## 2. Create a Firebase Project

1. Visit: https://console.firebase.google.com
2. Create a new project  
3. Add a Web App  
4. Copy the Firebase configuration snippet  

---

## 3. Enable Email and Password Authentication

Inside Firebase:

1. Go to **Build**  
2. Go to **Authentication**  
3. Select **Sign-in Method**  
4. Enable **Email/Password**  

---

## 4. Create Your firebase.js File

Create a new file in your **src** folder and name it **firebase.js**

Paste your Firebase configuration snippet from the Firebase Console and set up Firebase Auth:

```
import { initializeApp } from "firebase/app";
import { getAuth } from "firebase/auth";

const firebaseConfig = {
  apiKey: "YOUR_API_KEY",
  authDomain: "YOUR_AUTH_DOMAIN",
  projectId: "YOUR_PROJECT_ID",
  appId: "YOUR_APP_ID"
};

const app = initializeApp(firebaseConfig);
export const auth = getAuth(app);
```

Replace the placeholder values with your real Firebase project configuration.

---

## 5. Full Example: Simple Login and Register Page

Copy this into your **App.jsx**:

```
import { useState } from "react";
import { auth } from "./firebase";
import {
  createUserWithEmailAndPassword,
  signInWithEmailAndPassword
} from "firebase/auth";

export default function App() {
  const [email, setEmail] = useState("");
  const [password, setPassword] = useState("");
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const [error, setError] = useState("");

  const register = async () => {
    try {
      const res = await createUserWithEmailAndPassword(auth, email, password);
      setUser(res.user);
      setError("");
    } catch (err) {
      setError(err.message);
    }
  };

  const login = async () => {
    try {
      const res = await signInWithEmailAndPassword(auth, email, password);
      setUser(res.user);
      setError("");
    } catch (err) {
      setError(err.message);
    }
  };

  const logout = () => {
    auth.signOut();
    setUser(null);
  };

  return (
    <section style={{ padding: "20px" }}>
      <h2>Simple Firebase Auth</h2>

      {!user && (
        <>
          <input
            type="email"
            placeholder="Email"
            onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)}
            autoComplete="email"
            id="email"
          />

          <input
            type="password"
            placeholder="Password"
            id="password"
            onChange={(e) => setPassword(e.target.value)}
          />

          <button onClick={login}>Login</button>
          <button onClick={register}>Register</button>

          {error && <p style={{ color: "red" }}>{error}</p>}
        </>
      )}

      {user && (
        <section>
          <h3>Logged in as: {user.email}</h3>
          <button onClick={logout}>Logout</button>
        </section>
      )}
    </section>
  );
}
```

---

## 6. How the Authentication Works

### Main Firebase Functions Used:

- **createUserWithEmailAndPassword** → registers a new user  
- **signInWithEmailAndPassword** → logs in an existing user  
- **signOut** → logs the user out  

---

## 7. Summary

This tutorial covered:

- Installing Firebase  
- Setting up a Firebase project  
- Enabling Email/Password authentication  
- Creating a basic React page to log in and register users  

You now have a fully working Firebase authentication starter!

The official Next.js documentation already does a good job explaining how to use CSS-in-JS libraries like styled-components with the App Router. One piece that can still feel confusing, however, is 
**why a ThemeProvider is needed and where it should live**.

This post focuses only on that part.

## Why use a ThemeProvider?

A ThemeProvider allows you to define a shared set of design values (colors, shadows, spacing, etc.) and access them inside styled-components through the *theme prop*.

Instead of hardcoding values in every component, you define them once and reuse them consistently across your app.

## Where the ThemeProvider belongs in the App Router

With the App Router, layouts are rendered on the server by default. Since styled-components relies on client-side rendering for styles, the ThemeProvider must be added inside a **client component**.

A common pattern is to create a small provider wrapper and use it in the root layout.

### Example theme file

Here is a simple theme object:
```
    export const lightTheme = {
        colors: {
          background: "#ffffff",
          text: "#1f1f1f",
          border: "#eaeaea",
        },
      };
```
## Creating a provider component

The provider itself must be a client component:
```
    "use client";
      import { ThemeProvider } from "styled-components";
      import { lightTheme } from "@/themes/light";
      export default function Providers({ children }) {
        return (
          <ThemeProvider theme={lightTheme}>
            {children}
          </ThemeProvider>
        );
      }
```
## Using the provider in the root layout

The provider is then wrapped around the app inside `layout.tsx`:
```
    import Providers from "@/components/Providers";

      export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
        return (
          <html lang="en">
            <body>
              <Providers>
                {children}
              </Providers>
            </body>
          </html>
        );
      }
```
Once this is in place, any styled-component in the app can access the theme through the

`theme` prop.

## Final thoughts

The Next.js documentation explains the setup in detail, but the key takeaway is simple: the ThemeProvider must live inside a client component and be applied at the layout level. Once that is done, styled-components and theming work consistently across pages.
